
# LanCache-NG (https://github.com/wiki-mod/lancache-ng)
# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
# Checked-in production template. For manual production installs, copy this to
# deploy/prod/.env.local, edit the untracked copy, and point manual compose
# commands at that file. setup.sh update/backup/restore/update-ip prefers
# deploy/prod/.env.local automatically when it exists.
#
# IPs bound to each mode's services.
# The server needs two LAN IPs. Add the second with:
#   ip addr add 192.168.234.11/24 dev eth0
# To make it permanent, add it to /etc/network/interfaces or netplan.

IP_STANDARD=192.168.234.10
IP_SSL=192.168.234.11

# Shared TSIG key for Kea DDNS → PowerDNS updates. Generated by setup.sh.
# Keep this secret — it allows dynamic DNS updates to the LAN zone.
DDNS_TSIG_KEY=

# Internal NATS service accounts. setup.sh generates these automatically.
# NATS_DNS_REPLICA_* is the primary's own co-located dns-ssl container --
# not a registered secondary. Registered secondaries each get their own
# per-instance NATS credential via auth callout instead (issue #583), issued
# at registration time and never stored in this file. NATS_CALLOUT_* is the
# Admin UI's own connection identity for answering those auth-callout
# requests.
NATS_UI_USER=lancache-ui
NATS_UI_PASSWORD=
NATS_DNS_WRITER_USER=lancache-dns-writer
NATS_DNS_WRITER_PASSWORD=
NATS_DNS_REPLICA_USER=lancache-dns-replica
NATS_DNS_REPLICA_PASSWORD=
NATS_CALLOUT_USER=lancache-nats-callout
NATS_CALLOUT_PASSWORD=
# System-account identity (issue #681): used only to look up and force-disconnect
# a removed/rotated secondary's live NATS connection. setup.sh generates a real
# password; REQUIRED, same as the other static NATS roles above.
NATS_SYS_USER=lancache-nats-sys
NATS_SYS_PASSWORD=

# Token used by setup.sh secondary to register new secondary DNS nodes.
# REQUIRED even if you never register a secondary DNS node: the ui
# container's boot-time check refuses to start on an empty or placeholder
# value (an empty token used to allow unauthenticated secondary
# registration -- see issue #659). Only the one registration route is
# actually disabled if you skip secondary DNS; the token itself is not
# optional, matching setup.sh's own unconditional generation of this value.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
SECONDARY_REGISTRATION_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME_SECONDARY_REGISTRATION_TOKEN

# Shared PowerDNS API key for DNS containers and Admin UI.
# Generated by setup.sh; or set manually with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Keep secret — this key controls the PowerDNS HTTP API.
PDNS_API_KEY=

# Shared token for Netdata's alarm-notify.sh to authenticate to the Admin
# UI's POST /api/netdata-alarms webhook (bug hunt #849, observability.md
# finding #3: forwards Netdata's health.d alarms into the dashboard).
# Left empty here on purpose, same as PDNS_API_KEY above: both the netdata
# and ui containers resolve the real value from the shared-secrets volume
# (issue #858) if this stays empty. Generate manually with:
# openssl rand -hex 32
NETDATA_ALARM_TOKEN=

# Kea Control Agent token shared by DHCP and Admin UI.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
KEA_CTRL_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME_KEA_CTRL_TOKEN
# What: Kea snapshot dir inside the dhcp and ui containers
# Why: UI writes, dhcp rescue mode reads; one shared path
# From: Issue #1683 | PR #1858
KEA_CONFIG_SNAPSHOT_DIR=/var/lib/kea/config-snapshots

# Admin UI authentication.
# Set both values to enable Basic Auth.
# If both are empty, set ALLOW_INSECURE_UI=true only for an explicitly unauthenticated UI.
UI_AUTH_USER=
UI_AUTH_PASSWORD=
ALLOW_INSECURE_UI=false

# What: image source; TAG is latest, nightly or vX.Y.Z
# Why: latest is built from master, nightly from current_dev
# From: Issue #1683 | PR #1858
LANCACHE_IMAGE_REGISTRY=ghcr.io
LANCACHE_IMAGE_PREFIX=wiki-mod/lancache-ng
LANCACHE_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# Production bind-mount state root.
# New installs use /opt/lancache-ng. Existing repository-based installs that
# already have state elsewhere should set this one root before the first manual
# compose run after upgrading, then leave the per-service overrides unset unless
# they intentionally split state across multiple disks.
LANCACHE_STATE_DIR=/opt/lancache-ng

# What: proxy cache size, RAM keys zone and validity
# Why: compose maps these into proxy; one owner here
# From: Issue #1683 | PR #1858
CACHE_MAX_SIZE=50g
CACHE_MEM_MB=512
CACHE_SLICE_SIZE=8m
CACHE_VALID_HIT=365d
CACHE_VALID_ANY=1m
CACHE_INACTIVE=365d
# What: real upstream DNS for nginx; IPv6 bracketed
# Why: the LanCache DNS would loop back (AG-OP-002)
NGINX_UPSTREAM_RESOLVER=8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4 [2001:4860:4860::8888] [2001:4860:4860::8844]
# What: lazy proxies any host; strict only cdn-domains
# Why: lazy keeps new CDN names working (AG-OP-003)
PROXY_SECURITY_MODE=lazy
# What: optional client CIDR allowlist; empty allows all
# Why: clients are otherwise limited by the bound ports
PROXY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_CIDRS=

# What: Kea pool, used with the dhcp-kea profile
# Why: compose maps these into dhcp; one owner here
# From: Issue #1683 | PR #1858
DHCP_SUBNET=10.0.0.0/24
DHCP_RANGE_START=10.0.0.128
DHCP_RANGE_END=10.0.0.254
DHCP_GATEWAY=10.0.0.1

# What: dnsmasq proxy/relay values; Admin UI may override
# Why: compose maps these into dhcp-proxy; one owner here
# From: Issue #1683 | PR #1858
DHCP_SUBNET_START=
DHCP_DNS_PRIMARY=
DHCP_DNS_SECONDARY=
UPSTREAM_DHCP_IP=
# What: relay mode only: this relay's client-side IP
# Why: dnsmasq stamps it as giaddr for the upstream pool
# From: Issue #844
DHCP_RELAY_LOCAL_ADDR=
# What: optional PXE-scoped options; empty = not sent
# Why: ProxyDHCP works with the four values above alone
# From: Issue #450
DHCP_PROXY_INTERFACE=
DHCP_PROXY_ROUTER=
DHCP_NTP_SERVERS=
DHCP_PROXY_DOMAIN=
DHCP_PROXY_BOOT_FILENAME=
DHCP_PROXY_BOOT_SERVER=
DHCP_PROXY_CUSTOM_OPTIONS=
# What: PXE boot pointer; needs server plus a filename
# Why: dnsmasq starts replying to PXE clients once set
# From: Issue #705
DHCP_PROXY_PXE_BOOT_SERVER=
DHCP_PROXY_PXE_BOOT_FILENAME_BIOS=
DHCP_PROXY_PXE_BOOT_FILENAME_UEFI=

# Optional per-service state overrides. Normally keep these commented so the
# compose file derives every state mount from LANCACHE_STATE_DIR.
# CACHE_DIR is the one cache-path override as of v0.2.0 (issue #445). The old
# split CACHE_DIR_STANDARD/CACHE_DIR_SSL keys are EOL: no compose file reads
# them anymore, so setting either one by hand in this file is a silent no-op.
# setup.sh update still reads them exactly once, to migrate a pre-v0.2.0
# install's existing path into CACHE_DIR, then deletes both keys.
# CACHE_DIR=/custom/cache
# setup.sh update only writes the keys below when it must preserve a real
# legacy/custom path that no longer matches the shared state root.
# PDNS_STANDARD_DIR=/custom/pdns-standard
# PDNS_SSL_DIR=/custom/pdns-ssl
# PDNS_FILTER_STATE_DIR=/custom/pdns-filter-state
# KEA_DATA_DIR=/custom/kea
# NATS_DATA_DIR=/custom/nats
# NATS_CONF_DIR=/custom/nats-conf
# SYSLOG_NG_LOG_DIR=/custom/syslog-ng

# Central logging (#453). This checked-in template stays opt-in via
# --profile logging (or COMPOSE_PROFILES=logging in your own .env.local),
# same as the dhcp-kea/dhcp-proxy/ntp profiles above/below -- none of this
# manual production path's optional profiles start unless you ask for them
# explicitly. This is narrower than the setup.sh wizard's behavior since
# issue #1343: a fresh wizard install (deploy/quickstart, deploy/full-setup)
# defaults "Enable central logging?" to Y, and `setup.sh update` run against
# an EXISTING deploy/prod install (via .env.local) converges LOGGING_ENABLED
# to "1" the same way it does for any other install, since that migration
# path doesn't distinguish install type. A from-scratch manual checkout that
# never runs setup.sh at all gets no such default and must opt in here
# itself. ALSO set LOGGING_ENABLED=1 below when opting in this way: it is
# the separate flag the watchdog binary (services/watchdog) reads to decide whether
# the syslog+fluent-bit container is part of this stack for alert-only
# health monitoring -- starting the profile without it still runs the
# container correctly, but watchdog silently omits it from monitoring and
# the Admin UI dashboard's service list. The reverse mismatch is worse, not
# merely asymmetric: setting LOGGING_ENABLED=1 here WITHOUT also enabling
# the `logging` profile makes watchdog probe a syslog container that was
# never started -- it reports "unreachable" every check, which is treated
# as a failure with no cap or auto-reset (alert-only monitoring never takes
# a corrective restart action, so nothing ever clears the alert), producing
# a permanent false-positive dashboard alert until an operator notices and
# fixes the mismatch. Keep both settings in sync in either direction. Safe
# defaults below; only uncomment to change the rotation threshold or
# compression level.
# LOGGING_ENABLED=1
# SYSLOG_MAX_FILE_MB=100
# SYSLOG_COMPRESSION_LEVEL=19
# SYSLOG_NG_ROTATE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# Syslog storage-budget retention engine (#633; retention.sh's
# maybe_prune_syslog(), extracted from watchdog.sh into its own dedicated
# `retention` service/container by #842 Part 1, PR #1360). This is a
# DOUBLE opt-in: SYSLOG_ENABLED must be explicitly set to "true" below. The
# `retention` container that runs this engine always runs (it has no
# compose `profiles:` gate, unlike `syslog`/
# `syslog-ng`), so starting `--profile logging` alone does NOT enable
# pruning -- it only starts the log producers that create the tree pruning
# would act on. This is deliberate so turning on central logging never
# starts deleting files an operator didn't separately ask to have pruned.
# When enabled: files older than SYSLOG_RETENTION_DAYS are deleted first; if
# the tree is still over SYSLOG_MAX_GB afterward, the oldest remaining files
# are deleted next (regardless of age) until back under budget -- size
# budget takes priority over retention days.
# SYSLOG_ENABLED=false
# SYSLOG_MAX_GB=10
# SYSLOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Fluent-bit self-log rotation (#1236; retention.sh's
# maybe_rotate_fluent_bit_selflog(), see the note above -- also extracted
# from watchdog.sh into the `retention` service by #842 Part 1). Deliberately NOT part of the
# SYSLOG_ENABLED double opt-in above: that gate protects the syslog-ng
# OUTPUT tree (operator log data an operator explicitly asked watchdog to
# manage), while this bounds fluent-bit's OWN operational log
# (/data/fluent-bit.log, connection/flush retry noise during a syslog-ng
# outage) on its own volume -- a disk-safety concern that applies whenever
# `--profile logging` is active at all, regardless of whether SYSLOG_ENABLED
# is set. The live file is always archived (compressed) before truncation,
# never dropped, so nothing an operator needs to see during an outage is
# lost -- only moved into a rotated backup, same as syslog-ng's own rotation
# below. Always active once the `logging` profile creates the file; no
# separate enable flag exists.
# FLUENT_BIT_SELFLOG_MAX_MB=20
# FLUENT_BIT_SELFLOG_MAX_ROTATIONS=5
